{"name":"Re-Entry Red","hex":"#cd0317","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"entry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of entering.","Permission to enter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/entry"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atmospheric entry","blocks":[{"text":"Atmospheric entry is the movement of an object from outer space into and through the gases of an atmosphere of a planet, dwarf planet, or natural satellite. Atmospheric entry may be uncontrolled entry, as in the entry of astronomical objects, space debris, or bolides, or it may be controlled entry of a spacecraft that can be navigated or follow a predetermined course. Methods for controlled atmospheric entry, descent, and landing of spacecraft are collectively termed as EDL."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Entry.jpg/330px-Entry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmospheric_entry"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png/500px-Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png/1280px-Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png/500px-Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early reentry-vehicle concepts visualized in shadowgraphs of high speed wind tunnel tests","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlunt_body_reentry_shapes.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif/500px-Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif/500px-Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical Space Shuttle reentry profile","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATypical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Apollo_cm.jpg/500px-Apollo_cm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Apollo_cm.jpg/1280px-Apollo_cm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Apollo_cm.jpg/500px-Apollo_cm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Apollo command module flying with the blunt end of the heat shield at a non-zero angle of attack in order to establish a lifting entry and control the landing site (artistic rendition)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApollo_cm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mk_2.jpg/500px-Mk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mk_2.jpg/1280px-Mk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mk_2.jpg/500px-Mk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Prototype of the Mk-2 Reentry Vehicle (RV), based on blunt body theory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMk_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg/330px-Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg/330px-Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mk-6 RV, Cold War weapon and ancestor to most of the U.S. missile entry vehicles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Rv_film_pod.jpg/250px-Rv_film_pod.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content